--- a/doc/source/mac-layer.rst
+++ b/doc/source/mac-layer.rst
@@ -440,6 +440,69 @@ from the processing timings that are defined in the 'NR' module as:
 The values of the processing delays depend on the UE capability (1 or 2) and the configured numerology.
 Typical values for N1 are 1 and 2 slots, while N2 can range from 1 to 3 slots based on the numerology and the UE capability. The processing times are defined in Table 5.3-1/2 for N1 and Table 6.4-1/2 for N2 of [TS38214]_. Although in the standard they are defined in multiples of the OFDM symbol, in the simulator we define them in multiples of slots, because then they are used to compute dynamic K values that are measured in slots. Also note that N0 is not defined in the specs, but so is K0, and so we have included both in the 'NR' module.
 The values of the processing delays in the 'NR' simulator can be configured by the user through the attributes ``N0Delay``, ``N1Delay``, and ``N2Delay``, and default to 0 slots, 2 slots, and 2 slots, respectively.
+
+For a fixed cell-specific NTN scheduling offset, configure
+``NrGnbPhy::CellSpecificKoffset`` before starting the simulation. Values 1-1023
+are in 15 kHz reference slots (1 ms), following TS 38.331, clause 6.3.2,
+``NTN-Config``. The default value 0 represents an absent offset. For numerology
+``mu``, the added delay is ``CellSpecificKoffset * 2^mu`` active slots, following
+TS 38.213, clauses 8.3 and 9. For example, a value of 40 adds 40 slots at
+numerology 0 and 80 slots at numerology 1. It is separate from the N1/N2 UE
+processing delays.
+
+The offset enters the timing-map calculation for K1 and K2, including the RAR
+grant for Msg3. Slot selection still follows the configured TDD pattern; K0
+is unchanged. Configure the same reference offset on the paired BWPs when
+using FDD. The scheduler extends its existing DL/UL HARQ expiry window by the
+converted offset, retaining the same process count. The age counter counts
+scheduling indications in each direction, so expiry in a sparse TDD pattern
+can take longer than the corresponding number of elapsed slots. Retransmissions
+restart this window, and missing feedback still expires. At offset 0 the
+previous timing and expiry behavior are preserved.
+
+Under physical propagation delay the UE's slot clock lags the gNB's by the
+one-way delay, so without a timing advance every uplink transmission reaches
+the gNB one round-trip time after the slot it was scheduled for.
+``NrUePhy::TimingAdvance`` (a ``Time``, default 0) is the ideal timing advance:
+the UE's estimate of the one-way propagation delay to its serving gNB, which a
+Rel-17 NTN UE derives from GNSS and the SIB19 ephemeris and a simulation takes
+from the geometry (it can be updated at run time as the satellite moves). With
+it, the UE's slot grid starts every slot ``TimingAdvance`` before the gNB's, so
+UL data, UL control (HARQ feedback, CSI, SR), SRS and PRACH land in the gNB slot
+they were scheduled for, and the K1, K2 and Msg3 offsets carried by downlink
+messages are counted from the gNB slot that carried them (``floor((now -
+TimingAdvance) / slot)``, so a delay that is not a whole number of slots is
+handled) instead of the UE slot in which they arrived. ``CellSpecificKoffset``
+must cover the round trip, ``2 * TimingAdvance``, for the grants to be usable;
+a grant that points at a slot the UE has already transmitted is dropped with a
+warning. In FDD the attribute has to be set on every BWP PHY of the UE. With
+``TimingAdvance`` at 0 the terrestrial timeline is unchanged.
+
+A non-zero offset delays every uplink leg of the RRC connection procedure, so
+the connection timers have to be configured with it. Msg3, which carries the
+RRC CONNECTION REQUEST, and the RRC CONNECTION SETUP COMPLETE each wait for a
+grant delayed by the offset, while the downlink messages keep terrestrial
+timing because K0 is unchanged. With ``CellSpecificKoffset`` set to ``K``
+reference slots, the timers have to satisfy, at any numerology:
+
+* ``NrGnbRrc::ConnectionRequestTimeoutDuration`` >= 15 ms + ``K`` ms. It starts
+  when the RA preamble is received and covers the RAR and Msg3. Its upper bound
+  is 2000 ms, matching the maximum T300, so it can hold the maximum offset.
+* ``NrGnbRrc::ConnectionSetupTimeoutDuration`` >= 150 ms + ``K`` ms. It starts
+  when the RRC CONNECTION SETUP is sent and covers the setup-complete grant.
+* ``NrUeRrc::T300`` >= 100 ms + ``K`` ms, and never below the gNB's
+  connection-request timer. It starts when the UE sends Msg3.
+
+These settings are only required with the real RRC protocol
+(``NrHelper::UseIdealRrc`` set to false); the ideal protocol delivers the
+connection request without an uplink grant and never observes the offset. The
+defaults of 15 ms, 150 ms and 100 ms remain correct for a terrestrial cell.
+
+This is a scheduling model with slot-synchronous PHY timing (ideal timing
+advance). It does not implement SIB19 transmission, per-UE differential offsets,
+K_mac, or propagation-dependent timing advance. Runtime changes to the offset
+with outstanding grants are not supported.
+
 The allowed ranges are bounded by the attribute checkers: N0 can be 0 or 1 slot, while N1 and N2 can range from 0 to 4 slots.
 
 For the scheduling timings let us note that each K cannot take a value smaller than
@@ -620,6 +683,23 @@ each of the messages and signals described in the specs [TS36321]_.
      transmission of the CR MAC CE are not modeled.
 
 
+The UE arms its RA response timeout ``SlotPeriod * (6 + RaResponseWindowSize)``
+after the preamble (9 ms by default at numerology 0) and retransmits the
+preamble when it fires, while the RAR only comes back one round-trip time plus
+the gNB's RAR latency later. ``NrUeMac::IdealRandomAccess`` (default false)
+never arms the timeout, so random access completes on the first RAR whatever
+the propagation delay (Msg1 and Msg2 still cross the channel); since a preamble
+the gNB cannot fit a Msg3 grant for is only retransmitted by the timeout, it is
+meant for scenarios in which every preamble is answered. When it is false
+(the default), the timeout fires ``2 * NrUePhy::TimingAdvance`` later than the
+terrestrial value, i.e. the window is extended by the UE-gNB round-trip time as
+in TS 38.213, clause 8, for a cell with ``NTN-Config`` (Rel-17); the RAR is
+accepted from the first slot after the preamble in both cases, so an early
+response is never rejected. Note that
+``NrGnbRrc::ConnectionRequestTimeoutDuration`` runs even with ideal RRC: the gNB
+arms it when it creates the UE context on preamble reception, before the RAR
+leaves, so it has to exceed the round-trip time plus the RAR latency.
+
 Figure :ref:`fig-mac-random-access-contention` and
